100	- 2026-09-25, on the wording of protocol § 6's three rules after an Opus tone review of the coordinator's reply that proposed them (slogans, absolutes, passive voice on the failure, a shape offered with no alternatives): "Leave protocol as is" — the plain wording committed in `38829a636` stands. And the session's model is switched to Opus after this compaction at his request, "to get a feel for it"; he wants to consider pinning the workflow's escalated judge, which today inherits the session's model, explicitly to Fable, a note for the coordinator after the compaction to put to him.
101	- 2026-09-25, after the diagonal probe's timings came out about twice the 09-15 captures for no known reason: "we should always instruct workers when they record timings to record also the CPU so that we can guesstimate the comparisons" — protocol § 6.
102	- 2026-09-25, row 40 of the re-approval table (the three questions of `coordinator/array-design.md` left at the worker's defaults on 2026-09-19: a store per element type chosen by the factory under the library's generic objects, the size also carried as a field on the store, a factory for a size known only at run time returning the unsized `Array`), after it was re-explained with the library's own way first, what changed since and the peers: "close as no decision taken". None of the three stands as a default; nothing is reverted, since nothing was built; the three questions return with the array design after the switch-over, through protocol § 6's method (a clean worker's list of every way the language and the library offer, the library's own way first, the nine steps), with the review's findings (`reviews/array-design-review.md`: the first refuted by probe, the second right and forced, the third stopping microGPT at the checker) and the ground moved since (one library; design B, whose factory can make a size's descriptor from a number known only at run time, `perf-probes/nat/size-rung-probes.md`) as their input. The same hour he asked why microGPT uses `RR64` and not `RR32`, his premise being that LLMs are trained at FP32 at most; the reason on record is the Python reference's float64 goldens (`experiment/run-c-goldens/README.md`); adding the element width to the returning array questions was recommended and is not yet answered.
103	- 2026-09-26, row 47 of the re-approval table (climb batch 2's gate judge changing the `testSystem` comparison from shard by shard to the sum of the four shards, and deferring a comparison of test names): "Agreed with recommendation" — the sum stays for batch 4, as batches 3 and 3.5 ran; the name comparison (red when a test that passed before is missing or failing) comes later as a script change put to him on its own. Row 48 (the last landed summary as the comparand) was not taken; batch 4 runs under it as it stands. In the same message: "Let's run batch 4 overnight", taken as the go for a manifest held to the items he decided on 2026-09-24 (the interpreter coercion rung, the specification rung with its decision record, the size wall's checker rung with `keep-size-params.patch`, rows 379 and 380/381 as rungs if they share no file), any rung needing a decision not on record held out for him.
104	- 2026-09-26, the judge-tier ask left for the coordinator after the session moved to Opus: "Second ruling is for Fable." — a judge's second ruling on the same rung or tree runs on Fable, named in `coordinator/climb-batch-workflow.js` (`judgeTier`), no longer the session's model; first rulings stay on Opus; Fable is spent only when a second ruling happens. `coordinator/repair-batch-workflow.js` is unchanged (not used for batch 4). And, in the same message, a Fable worker with his yes: "let Fable review coordinator/CLIMB-BATCH-4.md before we launch it" — the manifest is reviewed by one Fable worker after the Opus worker writing it lands, and the batch launches only after that review and the coordinator's fixes.
105	- 2026-09-26, on who commits a worker's output: "Workers should be committing themselves as they go, focused their files only. Protects against container failures and stops from harness bothering you about uncommitted files, that you are hiding under `.` replies." — replacing the coordinator's practice of briefing workers to commit nothing and committing their files after review; written into protocol § 5 with the push check the batch's gather needs, and sent to the two workers then running (S1, S2).
106	- 2026-09-26, climb batch 4's held push, rung C's stop (any interpreter test's output changing) met by three tests whose outputs differ only in Java line numbers (`XXXimmutableTopLevel`, through the two files C edited) and object identity hashes (`taskTrace2`, `taskTrace3`), identical once those are masked (`compile-ladder/rung-interp-coercion/JUDGE.md`, "For Pavol" item 1): "(a), push" — read as outside the stop's purpose (nothing converts in them); C, N and K land as gathered, and S1's note (`reviews/spec-change-form.md`, committed by its worker, whose push the permission system refused) is pushed with them. O stays stopped for his decision.
107	- 2026-09-26, on S2 of batch 4's specification rung (what the specification's examples refused by the multiple instantiation exclusion become): "Run them and let's finish that exploration properly. S2 judgement on Fable, go." — the eight probes the permission system kept the worker from running are run, the note is finished with them, and the judgement is made by one Fable worker under the two-step rule of 2026-09-20.
108	- 2026-09-26, rung O of climb batch 4 (row 379), after the clean list of `reviews/wrap-dependent-code.md`: "Note from O looks solid to me. Don't see any credible alternative, so unless you do let's put that on the record." — the note's option 1, the specification's own way: the wrapping operators ∔ `DOTPLUS`, ∸ `DOTMINUS` (unary and binary) and ⨰ `DOTTIMES` (`Specification/basic/operators/opr-overview.tex:172-176`, `:205-209`) are declared on `Integral[\I\]` and on `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `NN32`, `NN64` and `ZZ` in the interpreter's library, bound to wrapping natives of their own beside the checked ones; the library bodies that mean to wrap (the six `split-1` sites and the `+1` sites of `Library/RangeInternals.fss`, `Random.fss`'s `LinearCongruential`, `ChunkedSparseArray.fss:76`, `IntMap.fss:676`) and the five team tests that assert or rely on wrapping (`intPrim`, `longPrim`, `HeapTest`, `QuickCheckTest`, `ReflectiveQuickCheckTest`) are rewritten with them, every expression fully parenthesised since the operators' precedence is stated only among themselves, and the strided distance that must not overflow is reordered and keeps the checked operators; then the ten natives raise `IntegerOverflow` (rung O's `natives.patch`), with the count of changed interpreter tests measured again and expected at zero. The saturating family waits for a step of its own. This decides row 348 for the specification's spelling: at the switch-over the compiler prelude's reversed spellings, in 10 compiled test files, are respelled. The coordinator saw no credible alternative (the note's option 2 restructures every body with no operator and leaves the specification's spelling undeclared; option 3 adds the saturating family now, beyond the need). Open, for the rung's manifest: `NN32` and `NN64` wrap under walk as well (bare `iadd`/`lmul` natives), outside row 379's ten natives.
109	- 2026-09-26, S1 of climb batch 4's specification rung, how the revised specification shows a change (`reviews/spec-change-form.md`): "S1 — 1, teams' way." — the layered form the team used for 1.0 and in its 2009 draft: a labelled callout at each changed passage that prints in every build (not a `\note`, which would read as the team's and vanish in a release build); a revival section in the changes appendix (Appendix I, `Specification/appendices/changes.tex`), each entry with the reason, the original sentences quoted with their path and line in the unrevised copy, and route C with what reversing to it would take; one paragraph in the front matter; the full reasoning in a repository document. What the unrevised copy is called in those citations waits on the specification-lineage note (`coordinator/spec-lineage.md`, in flight), since `Specification-1.0-frozen/` holds the 2011 draft, not 1.0.
110	- 2026-09-26, on the lineage note (`coordinator/spec-lineage.md` with the coordinator's check): asked whether the Types chapter of the team's unfinished restart (`Documentation/Specification/Prose/Language/types.tick`, Luchangco, 2012-05-31) counts as the designers' later word wherever it covers something, he quoted the recommendation, "on the rule S revises, it is the designers' latest intent, and the goal is to finish what they intended", and answered "Agreed." So `Specification/` stays the standard, and the Types chapter is cited beside it wherever it covers a topic: the rule as "instantiation exclusion" in its two-part form, the `covariant` modifier, `Bottom` inexpressible. One sentence saying so was added to the goal in CLAUDE.md. The coordinator had withdrawn this ask at the boot that followed the compaction, reading it as covered by his rule of 2026-09-23. His answer takes the original yes. The S2 ask (the judgement's per-example verdicts for rung S) is still open.
111	- 2026-09-26, S2 of the specification rung (`reviews/spec-refused-examples-judgement.md`, the Fable judgement): "S2 yes, take the verdicts for rung S". Rung S, now in batch 5, revises each refused example to the one library's shape:
112	  - the where-clause section's empty list becomes `object Empty[\T\] extends List[\T\]`;
113	  - the covariance example becomes the library's widening function with bounded static parameters;
114	  - "a subtrait of every instantiation" stays as the counterexample that introduces the rule;
115	  - `convenience.tex`'s `Nothing` becomes `Nothing[\T\]`, with no `excludes`, and the exception chapters' defaults become `Nothing[\String\]` and `Nothing[\Exception\]`;
116	  - `Object` (and `Tuple`, whose prose leans on it) loses the algebraic supertraits, with `===` an operator on `Any`;
117	  - the internal appendix gets one sentence at each of its three passages;
118	  - each original is kept in the text as "Not allowed", the specification's own form (`basic/traits.tex:286-292`);
119	  - the rule is stated once, in Luchangco's two-part form under "instantiation exclusion": which instantiations exclude each other in `basic/types-vals-vars.tex`, the rule on declarations in `basic/traits.tex`;
120	  - the 2012 `covariant` keyword goes into the decision record.
121	  Asked the same hour whether the keyword is future work or on the critical path, he was told it is future work: no library declaration and no microGPT line uses it. It comes as two ledger entries among the S2 items: a defect row (the checker accepts it, the compiled run crashes, walk refuses) and worklist item 12 reworded to declaration-site variance by the keywords. Still open from the judgement's section 11, one per message:
122	  - the tower chapters' sequencing with the flattening rung;
123	  - whether sizes and boolean arguments count in the rule;
124	  - row 331 re-gated on static-argument inference;
125	  - worklist item 12;
126	  - `RationalQuantity`;
127	  - whether the keyword is mentioned in the text (default: record only);
128	  - the five candidate ledger rows;
129	  - what S1's citations call the unrevised copy.
130	  `CLIMB-BATCH-4.md`'s option (b) is corrected when S is briefed.
131	- 2026-09-26, the two ledger entries for the 2012 `covariant` keyword (the judgement's candidate row 1 and its § 8 proposal for worklist item 12), put to him as future work, not on the path to microGPT: "Agreed." Row 404 is appended, a soundness hole: the checker accepts variance and neither path carries it to run time. Its expected-failure tests are owed in a batch rung. Worklist item 12 is reworded to declaration-site variance by the keywords, carried to run time on both paths. In the same message he confirmed S2 ("Yes. Sure."). The judgement's candidate rows 2-5 are still open; its row 4 (sizes in the rule) is already row 402, opened by rung N.
132	- 2026-09-26, the number chapters under S2 (the judgement's § 3.3): "Agreed" to option 1. The tower chapters (`basic-lib/basic-integers.tex`, `basic-lib/numbers.tex`, `advanced-lib/numbers-advanced.tex`) change in the same batch as the flattening rung, never earlier. Rung S in a batch without the flattening rung leaves them untouched and lists them as deferred, with the reason. When the two share a batch, the gather checks the specification's declarations against the landed library. This narrows his 09-24 assignment for those chapters only. In the same message he asked whether everything held for the next batch should be split so that each step stays in sync, and asked for the project's state and an ordered plan of every open issue toward the running compiler: "I need you to help me with the high level judgment and plan coordination."
133	- 2026-09-26, the plan (the coordinator's page https://claude.ai/artifact/3CKVcCdTRC5SJA2hz5Fk5C, built from `coordinator/open-items-2026-09-26.md`): "Yes, this order and workers are go." The order is six phases:
134	  1. batch 5: the wrapping operators, rung S without the number chapters, and the run-time size rung;
135	  2. batch 6: the flattening, the number chapters, and rung O's natives;
136	  3. the checker at a true zero;
137	  4. the switch-over, natives then names;
138	  5. microGPT compiles: its static types, the array design and the code-generation holes;
139	  6. microGPT fast.
140	  His answers come in the page's order, one per message: five before batch 5, three before batch 6, four before the switch-over, the arrays after it. The re-approval rows and the other off-path items are parked until relevant; row 41 joins the switch-over's design. Four Opus workers go now:
141	  - W1, the true distance to the switch-over on shadow copies;
142	  - W2, a clean list with the nine steps for the specification's sentence that overloads may not differ in static parameters;
143	  - W3, the same for the `fill` pairs;
144	  - W4, the record repairs, with `PLAN.md` rewritten from the page.
145	- 2026-09-26, the first of the batch-5 answers, what rung S's citations call the unrevised copy: "Option 1, agreed." It is called "the Working Draft of February 2011", in the team's own form, since the 1.0 release named its predecessor by title and version ("Fortress Language Specification, Version 1.0β"). Each citation gives the path and line in `Specification-1.0-frozen/`, which stays untouched. The revival section of Appendix I says once that the directory's name is misleading: its sources are the working draft of 2011-02-02, and only `fortress.1.0.pdf` in it is the 1.0 release.
146	- 2026-09-26, the second batch-5 answer, whether sizes and boolean arguments count in the exclusion rule: "Option 1, sizes count." Every static argument counts except operator arguments. Rung S states the rule that way. The run-time size rung's brief takes row 402's checker fix at `checkP` with its test, and measures whether any library declaration is newly refused. Booleans follow once the checker supports boolean parameters. In the same message: "Update protocol". The gate line of protocol § 6 was made timeless: the suites, the atomic runs, the ladder and the reported count, with the counts in the last landed gate summary.
147	- 2026-09-26, the third batch-5 answer, whether the specification's text mentions the 2012 `covariant` keyword: "Option 2, agreed." Rung S adds one sentence to the revival callout at the covariance example (`trait-parameters.tex:339-353`, now "Not allowed" with the widening function as the way). The sentence says that the type group's later design declares variance with a `covariant` modifier (`Documentation/Specification/Prose/Language/types.tick:320-339`), that it is implemented on neither path, and points to the decision record and row 404. This is the team's own practice of marking unimplemented design in the text, applied to the later Types chapter he ruled is the designers' later word. The coordinator recommended it over the judgement's default of record only.
148	- 2026-09-26, the fourth batch-5 answer, how a size gets its run-time descriptor under design B: "Agreed, (b) factory". `RTTIsize.of(n)` keeps one descriptor per number in a table, with no holder class. The run-time size rung is briefed with it and with the one-method hash fix, and it measures the table lookup's cost on a dispatched call. His question first: should type descriptors use the same pattern? The answer given: they already do where they can. A generic's descriptor class keeps a static `DICTIONARY` (a `RttiTupleMap`) and a factory that returns one descriptor per argument tuple (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/runtimeSystem/InstantiatingClassloader.java:2677-2720`). Only a declared type keeps a class, because its declaration gives it code. So (b) is the team's instantiation pattern applied to numbers. The coordinator's earlier wording, that (a) was the team's pattern for type arguments, was imprecise.
149	- 2026-09-26, the fifth batch-5 answer, `NN32` and `NN64` under walk: "Agreed, option 1". The unsigned types follow the same rule as the signed ones: overflow raises `IntegerOverflow`, as the specification (`opr-overview.tex:155`) and the compiled path (`CompilerBuiltin.fss:768`) already have it. The wrapping rung of batch 5 also rewrites the intended wraps in Steele's `ProjectFortress/tests/UnsignedTest.fss` (`:195-211`, unary `-` as 2^w minus a value, and the wrapping products) and any unsigned wraps in the library with ∸ and ⨰, keeping the values and changing no output. Rung O in batch 6 also checks the unsigned natives (add, subtract, multiply and negate at both widths) beside row 379's ten. With this, the five answers batch 5 needed are all given.
150	- 2026-09-26, the Fable review of batch 5's manifest: "Yes, [Fable] should review. Let's continue with the questions." When the manifest worker's draft lands, one Fable worker reviews `CLIMB-BATCH-5.md` in place and reports reasons only. Each version is committed and diffed, and the launch waits for his go.
151	- Recorded 2026-09-26 for the separate OpenAI analysis session: Pavol approved a bounded review of the agreed numerical hierarchy redesign through a scalar-to-matrix use case, then lifted read-only mode to contribute the analysis on its own branch from `main`. After trunk advanced he instructed the branch to fast-forward and the review to continue. This authorizes the review and its documentation commits, not a new implementation batch or a change to the agreed route. Claude Code transcript hooks do not apply to this runtime; it has no direct session-transcript access.
152	- 2026-09-26, on Astra's source review of route A through C4's scalar-to-matrix path (`reviews/numeric-hierarchy-integration-review.md`), asked whether it changes batch 5 (it does not): "Yes" to taking it into `main`, which was done by cherry-picking its commit `8eaec0022`. Its checks go into batch 6's briefs:
153	  - the generic `Vector`/`Matrix` bodies are checked per operation's required capability, with no blanket ring bound that would exclude integer arrays;
154	  - the `SUM` replacement is accepted on the empty sum's type as well as its value;
155	  - a tiny `PView`/transpose product, with its controls.
156	  One small Opus worker runs its three-call probe of row 388's container case now, before batch 6 is briefed. In the same message: "Run Fable judgement as well." One Fable worker makes the top-tier judgement on answers 9 (the overload sentence) and 10 (the `fill` pairs) and on rung N's decision 3, from W2's and W3's clean lists, rung N's record and the S2 judgement.
157	- 2026-09-26, climb batch 5 (`coordinator/CLIMB-BATCH-5.md`, reviewed by Fable in place at `63c85213b`): "Batch 5 go". Its rungs are D (the wrapping operators, the unsigned types with natives of their own), S (the specification without the number chapters) and Z (sizes at run time by the factory). The three small choices take their defaults: Q2 masks Java line numbers, identity hashes and moved library positions, with every masked difference listed for him; Q3 leaves row 366 for later; Q5 adds no route C entry to Proposed Features.
158	- 2026-09-26, answer 6, the specification's sign-refined number types (`RationalQuantity` and the basic chapters' subtype lists; `reviews/flattening-questions-ways.md` Question 3), after a briefing on why the design exists, the peers and how to judge it: "Option 1, agreed." Batch 6's specification piece rewrites the subtype lists of `basic-lib/numbers.tex` and `basic-lib/basic-integers.tex` as the library's run-time check methods (`check`/`check_star`, returning `Maybe`). `advanced-lib/numbers-advanced.tex` is kept word for word and marked as a superseded design, in the S1 form. No library or compiler work. His condition: "make sure we clearly record the original design", and when covariance lands, "try this", "resurrect this": the sign flags as covariant static parameters (the note's way 2, probe `Q3Phantom`). This is recorded as future work on worklist item 12 and row 404, and the specification's superseded-design note points to that road. He also asked whether other languages can express it or whether this is research territory. He was answered in chat: expressible as a library encoding with type-level computation (C++ templates, Haskell type families, Scala 3), natively in proof assistants and refinement-type systems; the Fortress form, native subtyping between instantiations ordered by flags together with multiple dispatch, has no mainstream counterpart; the covariant encoding is engineering, inferring the flags through arithmetic with no annotations is research.
159	- 2026-09-26, answer 8, mixed widths after the flattening (`reviews/flattening-questions-ways.md` Question 2): "Option 1, agreed." Each wider integer type declares a `coerce` from each narrower one, the compiler prelude's own pattern and the specification's ZZ32/ZZ64/ZZ128 example. `ZZ32` and integer literals coerce into `RR64` (exact). `ZZ64` into `RR64` stays explicit (lossy), which is his 09-22 rule of correcting the JVM's mistakes where precision matters. A generic call or range over mixed widths infers the narrowest type both sides coerce into, a promotion rule written into the specification's empty inference chapter. It is built with row 388's fix in the checker phase; until then such a call writes its static argument. The specification's `widens` is deferred. Batch 6's flattening declares the coercions, and no model line changes. By inference, this also ends walk's silent 32-bit arithmetic on a `ZZ64` variable holding a literal, since the typed binding then converts.
160	- 2026-09-26, answer 9, the specification's sentence that overloads may not differ in static parameters (`reviews/overloading-judgement.md` question 1): "Yes, agreed", then again "Agreed, the judgement's recommendation." The sentence goes. The specification is revised to the type group's 2011 model the checker runs, with the original kept as superseded text in the S1 form. The checker keeps the paper's three rules with two changes: the return-type rule is checked over every instance, and one narrow rule stays from the sentence, that two generic arms in the more-specific relation agree on their static parameters position by position (Java's overriding rule; it closes row 398). Walk dispatches by declared domains, which ends its dependence on declaration order. The library's refused families are repaired by its own devices (125 to 113 measured on a copy). It lands in phase 3, after the flattening, as one batch of four rungs: specification, checker, walk and library. No model line changes.
161	- 2026-09-26, answer 10, the `fill` pairs (`reviews/overloading-judgement.md` question 2): "Agreed, tabulate." The diamond is fixed by redeclaring `fill` in the leaf array traits, as the library does for `copy`, `map` and `replica`. The function form is renamed `tabulate` (after Scala's `Array.tabulate`), and `fill` keeps the value form, every peer's meaning of the word. The change covers about 30 library call sites, 11 test lines, the function row of the specification's arrays figure, and 12 lines of the microGPT vocabulary helpers; no model line changes. It is measured to clear all 95 `fill` refusals and both factory refusals (125 to 103). It lands in batch 6 beside the flattening if they share no declaration, else in phase 3.
162	- 2026-09-26, answer 12, rung N's decision 3 (`reviews/overloading-judgement.md` question 3): "Agreed, decision 3 stands." A size the call cannot fix is an error at that call. It reaches into overload sets: when the arm the rules would pick has a size the call cannot fix, the call is refused with the same "could not infer" error instead of the arm being dropped, which closes row 400. It is one small checker rung with two expected-failure tests, for batch 6 or the checker phase. Still to come with the switch-over's design: the dead sizes' diff (E3, 25 api declarations) and row 41. The judgement's section 10 items (the implicit bound, walk and an unused unknown size, `seq`'s device, Naden's instantiation) come with the phase 3 brief.
163	- 2026-09-26, answer 11, the measure of distance to the switch-over (`perf-probes/prelude/switch-over-distance.md`): "Agreed, option 1". The checker count stays in every gate, reported and never red on its own; this is his yes to the 09-23 change `a2b4809a5`, which had none. The full measurement (all apis and components, the compile path's setting) is run once by a worker after batch 6 lands and before phase 3 is briefed, then becomes a report-only stage of phase 3's gates, whose job is driving it down.
164	- 2026-09-26, answer 7 (what replaces `SUM`'s and `PROD`'s catch-all on `Number`): "Let's run our Fable worker on prep for answer 6. We'll later retroactively verify with Astra." Read as answer 7, since answer 6 is decided and 7 is the one sent to Astra. One Fable worker judges it, with two-path probes on private caches, into `reviews/sum-replacement-judgement.md`; Astra's verdict, when it comes, checks it afterwards. The permission system refused the first launch as a change to shared resources, since the brief had the worker push to `main` while batch 5 runs; his answer: "I'm giving explicit permission to push." The worker commits and pushes its own files, under the push check of protocol § 5.
165	- 2026-09-26, answer 7, what replaces `SUM`'s and `PROD`'s catch-all on `Number` (`reviews/sum-replacement-judgement.md`, the Fable judgement): "Agreed, option A". One generic sum and one generic product reduction over the library's algebra bounds, each joined with the element type's own operator and taking its identity from the static argument through the library's `() -> T` witness `typecase` (the device of `array1`). The bare forms `SUM e`, `SUM vm` and `DOT` keep their text; a clause form whose element type nothing at the call fixes writes its static argument, as the team's tests do. The two model lines shown to him as diffs are approved: `MicroGptFlat.fss:43` becomes `SUM[\ZZ32\][j <- 0#i] matCount(j)` and `:28`'s `BIG MAX[t <- z] t` becomes `BIG MAX z`, together, so the goldens are measured once (the judgement's alternative 1, deferring the second, is not taken). With them: `FlatArrays.fss:181`, five check-program lines, the APL program's same lines, 11 library sites and 32 test lines (edits to the team's tests, flagged), and one S1-form note at `reductions.tex:23-25`. It lands in batch 6's flattening rung. Teaching walk to accept an unwritten static argument erased to `Bottom` (alternative 2) is later work, not a condition of the rung, as recommended. Open: alternative 3, whether `BIG MAXN`, `BIG MINN` and `BIG MINMAXN` (no caller anywhere) are dropped or kept with each type's least and greatest element. The three defects found go to the ledger at batch 6's gather. Astra's verdict checks this afterwards.
